<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Squire Reading Stack 2: Historical Timeline</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@picocss/pico@1/css/pico.min.css" />
    <style>
      body {
        max-width: 880px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 32px 80px;
        position: relative;
        box-sizing: border-box;
        font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times, serif;
        color: #000000;
        background-color: #ffffff;
        line-height: 1.6;
        font-size: 12pt;
      }
      h1 {
        font-size: 24pt;
        text-align: center;
        margin-bottom: 24px;
        font-weight: bold;
        color: #000000;
        border-bottom: 1px solid #000000;
        padding-bottom: 10px;
      }
      h2 {
        font-size: 16pt;
        margin-top: 24px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
        font-weight: bold;
        color: #000000;
      }
      h3 {
        font-size: 14pt;
        margin-top: 20px;
        margin-bottom: 8px;
        font-weight: bold;
        color: #000000;
      }
      p {
        margin-bottom: 16px;
        text-align: justify;
      }
      .timeline-item {
        margin-bottom: 24px;
      }
      .timeline-date {
        font-weight: bold;
        text-decoration: underline;
      }
      .why-it-matters {
        font-style: italic;
        margin-top: 4px;
        display: block;
      }
      .study-box {
        border: 2px solid #000000;
        padding: 20px;
        margin: 32px 0;
        background-color: #fcfcfc;
      }
      .study-box h3 {
        margin-top: 0;
        text-align: center;
      }
      ul.checklist {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding-left: 0;
      }
      ul.checklist li {
        margin-bottom: 8px;
      }
      ul.checklist li::before {
        content: '[ ] ';
        font-family: monospace;
        margin-right: 8px;
      }
      ul.standard-list {
        padding-left: 40px;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
      }
      ul.standard-list li {
        margin-bottom: 8px;
      }
      hr {
        border: 0;
        border-top: 1px solid #000000;
        margin: 32px 0;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Squire Reading Stack 2: Historical Timeline</h1>

    <p>
      Welcome, Squires. This handout teaches the main historical timeline that you are expected to know for your
      advancement. Understanding these events in order will help you see how the order began, how it served the
      vulnerable, and how its original mission eventually ended. Please read these facts carefully, as they separate
      true history from simple legend.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>The Historical Timeline</h2>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">1095 - 1099:</span> The First Crusade</h3>
      <p>
        The First Crusade was a major event that happened before the order was formed. It was a journey by European
        Christians to secure the Holy Land, especially the city of Jerusalem. The region was very important to medieval
        Christians because of its holy sites.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> It created the setting in which Christian pilgrims began traveling to
        Jerusalem in large numbers, facing great danger along the way.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">After 1099:</span> Danger to Pilgrims</h3>
      <p>
        Even after the First Crusade ended and the Crusader states were established, the region remained very fragile.
        Travel on the roads was highly dangerous. Christian pilgrims traveling to Jerusalem were frequently robbed or
        attacked.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> It created the specific need for a dedicated order to protect these
        vulnerable travelers.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">Around 1119 or 1120:</span> The Founding</h3>
      <p>
        A knight named Hugh de Payns and his first companions formed a new group to protect the pilgrims. They
        established their early headquarters on the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, which gave the Templars their name.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> This marks the founding of the order, proving that it began with a
        mission of service and protection rather than a desire for power.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">Around 1128 or 1129:</span> The Council of Troyes</h3>
      <p>
        A formal church gathering called the Council of Troyes met to review the new group. At this council, the order
        received official church approval and formal support, establishing them as a recognized religious order, not
        just an ordinary army.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> It gave the order official legitimacy and structure.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">1139:</span> Papal Privileges</h3>
      <p>
        Pope Innocent II granted major privileges to the order. This gave the order strong official support from the
        church and showed that it had become an important part of the historical story.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> It strengthened the order greatly and recognized their importance to
        the Christian world.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">1291:</span> The Fall of Acre</h3>
      <p>
        The city of Acre fell, which was a major crisis for the Crusader states. This event weakened the order's
        original military purpose and marked an important turning point in its history.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> It weakened the order's original military purpose and created a severe
        strategic crisis.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">1307:</span> Arrests in France</h3>
      <p>
        The ruler of France, King Philip IV, ordered the arrest of the Templars in his country. By this time the order
        had become powerful, and power and influence can create enemies. This event began the final crisis of the
        order.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> This event shows how influence can create enemies and marks the
        beginning of the order's destruction.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">1312:</span> Suppression of the Order</h3>
      <p>
        Pope Clement V officially suppressed, or ended, the order in 1312. This was the formal church action that
        brought the historical order to its official end.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> This marks the formal and official end of the historical order.</span
      >
    </div>

    <div class="timeline-item">
      <h3><span class="timeline-date">1314:</span> Execution of Jacques de Molay</h3>
      <p>
        Jacques de Molay was the last Grand Master of the order. His execution in 1314 is the final major event in the
        main Squire timeline.
      </p>
      <span class="why-it-matters"
        ><strong>Why this date matters:</strong> It is the final major event in the historical timeline of the original
        order, representing a tragic but courageous end.</span
      >
    </div>

    <hr />

    <div class="study-box">
      <h3>How to Study This Timeline</h3>
      <p>Squires are expected to know these facts clearly for their advancement boards. Use these simple study tips:</p>
      <ul class="standard-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Learn the order of events:</strong> Do not just memorize random dates. Understand the story from the
          First Crusade to the final suppression.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connect names to actions:</strong> Know exactly what Hugh de Payns, Innocent II, Philip IV, and
          Clement V did.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Separate history from legend:</strong> Stick only to the facts presented here. Do not add unproven
          stories.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Practice out loud:</strong> Try explaining <em>why</em> each date matters to a parent, mentor, or
          fellow Squire.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Core Facts to Remember</h2>
    <p>Use this checklist to test your memory before your oral review:</p>
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>1095-1099: The First Crusade</li>
      <li>Hugh de Payns: The Founder of the Order</li>
      <li>Temple Mount, Jerusalem: The Early Headquarters</li>
      <li>Council of Troyes (1128/1129): Official Church Approval</li>
      <li>Innocent II: The Pope who granted major privileges in 1139</li>
      <li>Acre: The city whose fall in 1291 weakened the original mission</li>
      <li>Philip IV: The King who ordered the arrests in France in 1307</li>
      <li>Clement V: The Pope who suppressed the order in 1312</li>
      <li>Jacques de Molay: The last Grand Master, executed in 1314</li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>
